   "passage": "**[1]** Along tropical coasts, mangrove forests once formed a thick green wall between land and sea. Over the past half-century, large areas have been cleared, mainly to make room for shrimp ponds and seaside buildings.\n\n**[2]** The consequences reach far beyond the loss of trees. Mangrove roots slow incoming waves; without them, storms push sea water further inland and wear away the shore. Young fish and crabs that shelter among the roots disappear, and so, in time, do the catches of local fishers. Moreover, the mud beneath mangroves stores large amounts of carbon, which is released when the forest is dug up.\n\n**[3]** In response, many communities have started replanting. Early projects often failed because seedlings of a single species were planted in neat rows on open mudflats, where waves soon washed them away. More recent efforts therefore begin by repairing the natural flow of water and then let several species return by themselves.\n\n**[4]** Replanting alone, however, cannot replace what is still being cut. Every year, more mature forest is lost than new seedlings can make up for.",
